TY  - JOUR
TI  - An Overview of the Therapeutic Aspect of Living Drugs Probiotics
AU  - V. Patravale
AU  - A. Joshi
JO  - Nutraceutical and Functional Foods in Disease Prevention
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.4018/978-1-5225-3267-5.ch001
AB  - The human body harbors ten times more bacterial cells than human cells. These bacterial cells form the human microbiome that plays a vital role in human health. An imbalance in the gut microbiome (i.e., dysbiosis) can result in various pathological conditions. This dysbiosis can be refurbished with the supplement of “probiotics.” Probiotics have thus been defined as non-pathogenic micro-organisms that, when ingested, exert a positive influence on host health or physiology. The most commonly used probiotic bacteria comes from two genera: Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium. Various research findings have proposed a correlation between the alteration of microbiota (composition/activity/density) with disorders like infectious diarrhea, inflammatory bowel diseases, obesity, to name a few. The ultimate effect of administration of probiotics on health or physiology is either direct or indirect. This chapter summarizes the concept of probiotics, their therapeutic aspect along with possible mechanism of action.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Gut Microbiome
AU  - S. Ashman
AU  - H. Krishnamurthy
JO  - Effects of Lifestyle on Men's Health
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-12-816665-9.00004-4
AB  - Abstract The gut microbiome discusses the role of the intestinal microbiota in human health and disease both in broad context of immunomodulation and in specific influence of inflammatory processes. Technological advances are highlighted, including a variety of scientific methods of assessing the composition of the intestinal microbiome and strengths and weaknesses of each method. The symbiotic relationship of the intestinal microbiome and the human host is central to understanding how each affects and depends on the other for mutual survival, from birth through adulthood and through mechanisms of diverse microbial exposure, environmental exposure, and dietary patterns. Attention is also given to mechanisms that precipitate chronic disease and interventions including dietary and supplemental probiotics, prebiotics, and phenolic compounds, as well as additional supplemental nutrients that have merit in addressing intestinal dysbiosis.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Disorders of intestinal transit
AU  - K. Hedayat
AU  - J. Lapraz
JO  - The Theory of Endobiogeny
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-12-816964-3.00010-9
AB  - Abstract Disorders of intestinal transit are disorders in which transit is too rapid or too slow. Both situations affect the consistency of stool and nutrient absorption and reclamation. Rapid transit disorders are disorders resulting in diarrhea. These disorders are rooted in dysfunction of the small intestines. Secretory diarrhea is an infectious diarrhea, in excessive water enters into the lumen. Stool is poorly formed. Malabsorption diarrhea is a hyperosmotic diarrhea. The inability to hydrolyze basic nutritional elements for absorption results in a concentration gradient in the intestinal lumen that draws water in for the purpose of dilution. This can be a primary disorder, or postinfectious due to damage to the structure of the intestinal villi. Rapid transit diarrhea occurs when the motricity of the intestines is too rapid for optimal regulation of liquid balance within the stool. Constipation is a condition in which too much liquid is absorbed from stool prior to evacuation. It can occur in a state of normal transit time or slow transit time. There are various secondary causes of constipation arising from regional or systemic disorders. An Endobiogenic assessment of the origin of stool consistency and evacuation allows for a personalized treatment using medicinal plants, diet, and psychological interventions.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Microbiome and Allogeneic Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation
AU  - A. Chilkulwar
AU  - D. Weber
AU  - R. Jenq
AU  - E. Holler
JO  - Immune Biology of Allogeneic Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-12-812630-1.00009-8
AB  - Abstract The microbiome has recently emerged as a modulator of a variety of clinical outcomes, particularly those that modulated by the immune system. In the allogeneic HSCT setting, several groups have linked the microbiome to patient outcomes, particularly acute intestinal graft-versus-host disease and infectious outcomes, but also disease relapse and immune reconstitution. A variety of mechanisms mediated by intestinal bacteria have been uncovered, including production of innate ligands, metabolites, and mucus degrading enzymes, leading to modulation of the innate and adaptive immune system as well as intestinal epithelial biology. Preventing damage to the microbiota by limiting use of antibiotics, as well as supplementing the microbiome using diet and prebiotic strategies or restoring bacteria via probiotics and fecal microbiome transplantation, may prove beneficial to further reduce toxicity and improve on the success of allogeneic HSCT.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
TI  - Potential Health-Promoting Effects of Probiotics in Dairy Beverages
AU  - A. Shori
AU  - A. Baba
AU  - P. Muniandy
JO  - Value-Added Ingredients and Enrichments of Beverages
PY  - 2019
DO  - 10.1016/b978-0-12-816687-1.00005-9
AB  - Abstract Functional foods have gained great importance as a result of increasing interest in foods and beverages that promote health and disease prevention. Fermented milk beverages not only provide refreshment and hydration but also play an important role in the human diet and can be used as a potential source of probiotics with beneficial health effects. The fermentation process helps to increase the shelf life of beverages, improves the nutritional value, and enhances the sensory properties. Nowadays, there have been several developments in the probiotics fermented milk beverages. Therefore, this review aims to describe the health benefits of probiotics and their role in fermented dairy beverages.
